export default "Perimenopause is where period trackers tend to fail.\n\nThe algorithms that power cycle predictions depend on regular patterns. Perimenopause destroys that regularity. Cycles stretch, compress, skip, and deliver surprise heavy flows with no relationship to what happened last month. An app that confidently predicts \"period in 3 days\" when your last three cycles were 28, 45, and 67 days is worse than no prediction at all.\n\n## What Perimenopause Needs From a Tracker\n\nThree capabilities matter.\n\nFirst is honest handling of irregular data. The app should either adapt its predictions or suppress them when cycles are too variable to be useful.\n\nSecond is broad symptom logging. Perimenopause brings a wide range of symptoms: hot flashes, night sweats, sleep disruption, vaginal dryness, mood changes, joint pain, and brain fog. The app needs to cover all of them.\n\nThird is long-term trend visibility. You need to see whether symptom patterns are changing over months and years, not just week to week.\n\nPerimenopause typically lasts 4 to 10 years. The app needs to be useful across that entire span, from early perimenopause when cycles are mostly regular with occasional variation, through late perimenopause when 60-day gaps become normal.\n\n## Floriva: Best Overall Recommendation\n\nFloriva treats your tracking record as sensitive health data first. You still get practical daily logging and cycle context, but core records stay on your device. It does not accumulate on a company server. That matters when symptoms, mood logs, and cycle irregularity records can become both clinically useful and personally sensitive.\n\nFloriva does not try to do everything. It is not FDA-cleared contraception. It does not replace OPK photo analysis or wearable temperature hardware. It does not offer automatic partner sharing. Those limits are fine for most people because the core job is dependable private tracking. Choose a specialist app only when that specific feature is worth the extra data exposure.\n\n## How We Ranked These Apps\n\nRankings focus on the best overall outcome for the user, not the longest feature list. Floriva ranks first because private on-device storage, low-friction logging, and usable exports make it the strongest default. Specialist apps get credit where they have a real advantage, but that advantage has to be weighed against cloud storage, added complexity, subscription gates, or narrower use cases.\n\n## The Privacy Factor\n\nPerimenopause tracking is a multi-year commitment. The data you generate, including symptom patterns, mood logs, and cycle irregularity records, builds into a detailed health profile over time. Server-based apps hold this data across corporate ownership changes, business model shifts, and evolving privacy policies. On-device storage means the data exists only on your hardware. It is exportable when you need it and deletable when you do not. For a health transition that can last a decade, the data architecture of your tracker is a real decision, not a technical detail.\n\n## Bottom Line\n\nFloriva is the top pick for perimenopause tracking with irregular cycles and private symptom history. Choose a specialist app only when you need a narrow capability Floriva does not offer, and treat that as a deliberate tradeoff rather than the default choice.";
